{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"x unit fact x unit fact","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md","fileName":"xUnit Fact.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"xUnit Fact","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 11:46\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Test-Driven Development and Unit Test Design]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Test-Driven Development and Unit Test Design","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"x unit fact x unit fact","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md","fileName":"xUnit Fact.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"xUnit Fact","heading":"xUnit Fact","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An xUnit fact is a parameterless test case representing one invariant behavior. A method marked with `Fact` is discovered by the test runner and passes or fails according to its assertions and any unhandled exception.\n\nA fact should communicate one coherent expectation and arrange only the state relevant to it. Several unrelated assertions in one method make a failure less precise and the test more expensive to understand.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Test-Driven Development and Unit Test Design","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"x unit fact x unit fact","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/xUnit Fact.md","fileName":"xUnit Fact.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"xUnit Fact","heading":"References